        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The start of the Mir 23/NASA 4 mission was marked by the launch of STS-81 in January 1997.  STS-81 rendezvoused with the Mir Space Station, delivering Jerry Linenger, the NASA Increment 4 astronaut, to Mir and returning the NASA 3 astronaut, John Blaha, to Earth.  The Russian Mir 23 crew launched in February 1997, joining Linenger on Mir, and replacing the Mir 22 crew.  The NASA 4 mission ended as Linenger was retrieved and returned to Earth after the STS-84 mission.  <P>While STS-81 was docked to the station, astronauts and cosmonauts transferred science samples and data collected by the Mir 22/NASA 3 crew from the Mir to the Shuttle, which returned them to Earth to be analyzed by researchers.  Crewmembers also transferred hardware and supplies to Mir in support of the health and well being of the Mir 23/NASA 4 crew, as well as to support future investigations on the station. Many of the resupply science items directly supported Mir 23/NASA 4 investigations. <P>Science conducted on Mir during the long-duration missions was diverse.  Research areas included human life sciences, fundamental biology to study animals and plants, biotechnology, materials sciences, and spacecraft structural dynamics and environment. Results assisted researchers in developing the procedures for operating on board the International Space Station.  Nearly all Shuttle-Mir investigations were joint efforts between the United States and Russia, with at least one researcher involved from each country. <P>Human life sciences research consisted of investigations designed to study the crewmembers' adaptation to weightlessness in terms of skeletal muscle and bone changes, coordination and balance, psychological interactions, immune system function, and metabolism. In addition, environmental factors were assessed, including air and water quality, and surface and crew microbiology. This set of investigations continued the characterization of the integrated human response to a prolonged presence in space. <P>Fundamental biology research was designed to study the effect of the space environment on plants. Prolonged exposure to microgravity provided an ideal opportunity to determine the role gravity plays in cell regulation and how low gravity affects the development and growth of organisms. Investigations in this discipline also characterized the radiation environment inside the Mir space station. <P>Included under the fundamental biology research area was a payload called Biological Research in a Canister-Mir (BRIC-MIR).  BRIC payloads consist of passive experiments contained within metal canisters that have foam padding used to dampen vibrations.  The BRIC-Mir payload was specifically designed to investigate the somatic embryogenesis of daylily plant cells.  BRIC-MIR was delivered to the Mir Space Station on STS-81 and returned 4.5 months later on STS-84.<P>Commercially initiated research was aimed at evaluating new technologies and techniques, using the Mir Space Station as a unique testing environment.  A better understanding of the characteristics of superconductors and protein crystal growth, and the development of biological and chemical systems through fluid processing in reduced gravity, assisted in the design and operations of the International Space Station (ISS). The knowledge gained also has the potential to significantly improve aspects of commercial processing on Earth. <P>One group of experiments specifically prepared NASA for building and operating the ISS. These investigations consisted of several technology demonstrations associated with human factors and maintenance of crew health and safety aboard the station. To improve the design and operation of the International Space Station, information on the Mir interior and exterior environments was gathered and the station's structure and design were evaluated. The investigations studied radio interference, particle impact on the station, docked configuration stability, water microbiological monitoring, and inventory management. <P>Microgravity research was aimed at advancing scientific understanding through research in biotechnology, fluid physics, combustion science, and materials science. The ambient acceleration and vibration environment of Mir was also characterized to support future research programs. <P>To better understand our Earth and solar system, research in ocean biochemistry, land surface hydrology, and meteorology was also performed.  Observation and documentation of transient natural and human-induced changes were accomplished with the use of hand-held cameras. Earth orbit allows documentation of atmospheric conditions, ecological changes, seasonal changes over long periods and ephemeral events. <P>As the NASA-Mir program continued, unique data were collected from experiments that had never been performed during such long stays in space. With international cooperation, the Space Shuttle has lived up to its name, delivering and returning long-duration crewmembers, precious research supplies and living essentials to a one-of-a-kind research platform, the Mir Space Station. The NASA 4 mission was one more step closer to increasing the understanding of many scientific areas and how they relate to space exploration technology. This growing body of knowledge will undoubtedly serve as a foundation for maintaining a continuous human presence in space.
        </div>

